{"significantly_better_performance": "\"Significantly better performance\" refers to the superior results obtained by DeepEnroll when compared to existing rule-based and machine learning models in the context of patient-trial matching, as evidenced by experimental outcomes."},
{"pre_training_techniques": "Pre-training techniques refer to methods used in machine learning to prepare models before the main training phase. These techniques involve initializing model parameters in a way that enhances the efficiency and effectiveness of subsequent training processes."},
{"pre_training_techniques__goal__provide_model_training_with_good_initializations": "The goal predicate in this context signifies the primary objective or purpose that pre-training techniques aim to achieve. It highlights the intended outcome or benefit that these techniques are designed to deliver during the model training process."},
{"provide_model_training_with_good_initializations": "Providing model training with good initializations means setting up the initial parameters of a model in a manner that facilitates faster and more accurate learning during the training phase. This involves configuring the model in a way that it starts from a advantageous point, thereby improving overall performance."},
{"language_model_pre_training_techniques": "Language model pre-training techniques refer to methods used to initially train language models on large datasets before fine-tuning them for specific tasks. These techniques aim to provide a robust starting point for models, enabling them to learn general language patterns and representations."},
{"language_model_pre_training_techniques__improve__performance_on_multiple_natural_language_processing_tasks": "The predicate \"improve\" in this context signifies the enhancement or augmentation of a model's capabilities. Specifically, it denotes the process by which pre-training techniques elevate the overall effectiveness and efficiency of language models, making them better equipped to handle various natural language processing tasks."},
