{"sequence_of_tokens": "The sequence of tokens refers to a series of individual elements or units of text, such as words or characters, that are processed together as a continuous string. In the context of the method described, these tokens are analyzed to extract specific types of features."},
{"sequence_of_tokens__extracts__word_level_features_and_character_level_features": "The predicate \"extracts\" indicates the action of deriving or obtaining specific information from the sequence of tokens. In this case, it involves pulling out detailed features that are relevant for recognizing numerical information within the tokens."},
{"word_level_features_and_character_level_features": "Word-level features and character-level features are the types of information extracted from the sequence of tokens. Word-level features might include recognition of scientific units or written numbers, while character-level features could involve identifying digits, sequences of digits, or specific suffixes within the tokens."},
{"word_level_features": "Word-level features refer to the characteristics extracted from individual words within a sequence of tokens. These features are utilized to identify specific types of information contained within the words."},
{"word_level_features__recognize__scientific_units_and_written_numbers": "The predicate \"recognize\" in this context means to identify or detect specific patterns or entities within the word-level features. It involves determining whether certain words belong to a predefined set or category."},
{"scientific_units_and_written_numbers": "Scientific units and written numbers are specific types of information that can be identified within word-level features. Scientific units include measurements like \"mcg\" or \"g/L,\" while written numbers are numeric values expressed in words."},
{"character_level_features": "Character-level features refer to the detailed attributes extracted from individual characters within a sequence of tokens. These features are crucial for recognizing specific patterns or elements within the text, such as numerical information."},
{"character_level_features__contain__digit_all_digits_or_suffix_st_nd_rd_th": "The predicate \"contain\" signifies that the subject, character-level features, includes or possesses certain elements. In this context, it indicates that the character-level features encompass specific types of characters or character sequences."},
{"digit_all_digits_or_suffix_st_nd_rd_th": "The objects\u2014digit, all digits, or suffix (st, nd, rd, th)\u2014represent the specific character types or sequences that can be found within the character-level features. These elements are important for identifying and extracting numerical information from the text."},
{"extraction_results": "The extraction results refer to the outcomes obtained from the process of recognizing and extracting numerical information from a sequence of tokens. This involves identifying word-level and character-level features that indicate the presence of numerical values and units."},
{"extraction_results__formulate__numerical_information_representation_with_standardization": "The predicate \"formulate\" in this context means to structure or organize the extraction results in a specific way. It involves converting the recognized numerical information into a standardized format that is consistent and easy to compare or analyze further."},
{"numerical_information_representation_with_standardization": "Numerical information representation with standardization is the structured and uniform way in which the extracted numerical data is presented. This includes converting numbers into floating-point values, ensuring units are in standard base units, and fixing the date type, making the data comparable and analyzable."},
{"numerical_information_representation": "Numerical information representation is a method used to standardize and organize numerical data extracted from sequences of tokens. It involves recognizing and categorizing word-level and character-level features to accurately represent numerical values and units."},
{"numerical_information_representation__includes__numbers_converting_into_floating_points_and_fixed_date_type_and_units_converting_into_standard_base_unit": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the numerical information representation method encompasses specific processes or components. In this context, it indicates that the method incorporates the conversion of numbers and units into standardized formats."},
{"numbers_converting_into_floating_points_and_fixed_date_type_and_units_converting_into_standard_base_unit": "The object \"numbers converting into floating points and fixed date type, and units converting into standard base unit\" describes the specific processes included in the numerical information representation. This involves transforming numbers into floating-point representations and fixed date types, as well as converting units into their standard base units to ensure consistency and comparability."},
{"numerical_information_comparison_method": "The numerical information comparison method is a technique designed to evaluate and compare numerical data extracted from electronic health records (EHR) against numerical information representations (NIR) learned from external sources or clinical contexts (ECs). This method involves standardizing numerical values and units, and then performing comparisons to determine relationships between the data."},
{"numerical_information_comparison_method__infer__entailment_between_nir_learned_from_ecs_and_numerical_information_in_ehr": "The predicate \"infer\" in this context refers to the process of deducing or deriving a conclusion about the relationship between two sets of numerical information. Specifically, it involves analyzing the extracted numerical data from EHRs and the NIRs to determine if there is a logical entailment or contradiction between them."},
{"entailment_between_nir_learned_from_ecs_and_numerical_information_in_ehr": "The object \"entailment between NIR learned from ECs and numerical information in EHR\" refers to the logical relationship that can be established between the numerical information representations derived from external clinical contexts and the numerical data found within electronic health records. This relationship can be categorized as \"entailment,\" \"contradiction,\" or \"not comparable,\" depending on the comparison results."},
{"numerical_information_in_ehr": "Numerical information in EHR refers to data within electronic health records that is expressed in a quantifiable format. This includes measurements, quantities, and other data points that can be represented numerically."},
{"numerical_information_in_ehr__often_represented_as__fixed_value_with_unit": "The phrase \"often represented as\" indicates a common way in which something is depicted or expressed. In this context, it describes how numerical information in EHR is typically formatted."},
{"fixed_value_with_unit": "A fixed value with unit is a specific numerical quantity accompanied by a unit of measurement. This representation ensures clarity and standardization, allowing for consistent interpretation and comparison of data across different records and systems."},
{"method": "The method described is a systematic approach for processing and comparing numerical information within electronic health records (EHR). It involves extracting features from tokens, standardizing numerical data, and comparing units and values to infer entailment.|Here is another description: The method refers to a numerical information comparison technique designed to infer the entailment between numerical information recognized from electronic case reports (ECs) and numerical information present in electronic health records (EHR). This method involves standardizing numerical data and comparing it against predefined value ranges."},
{"method__check__whether_two_units_are_comparable": "The predicate \"check\" in this context refers to the action of verifying or assessing whether two units are compatible for comparison. This is a crucial step in the method to ensure that subsequent comparisons of numerical values are meaningful and accurate."},
{"whether_two_units_are_comparable": "The object \"whether two units are comparable\" refers to the evaluation of the compatibility of units within numerical data. This evaluation is essential for determining if the numerical values associated with these units can be directly compared, which is a foundational step in the method's process of inferring entailment from numerical information."},
{"method__compare__fixed_value_with_the_value_range_in_nir": "The predicate \"compare\" in this context signifies the action of evaluating and assessing the relationship between the standardized numerical information extracted from EHR and the value ranges defined in the numerical information representation (NIR). This comparison aims to determine if the numerical data from EHR aligns with, contradicts, or is not comparable to the NIR."},
{"fixed_value_with_the_value_range_in_nir": "The object \"fixed value with the value range in NIR\" refers to the specific numerical values, along with their associated units, that are predefined within the numerical information representation (NIR). These fixed values serve as benchmarks against which the numerical information extracted from EHR is compared to ascertain its entailment status."},
{"entailment_results": "The entailment results refer to the outcomes generated by the numerical information comparison method designed to infer the relationship between numerical information in electronic case reports (ECs) and electronic health records (EHR). These results are crucial for determining whether the numerical data from ECs supports the information found in EHR."},
{"entailment_results__includes__entailment_contradiction_and_not_comparable": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the entailment results encompass or contain specific categories or outcomes. In this context, it indicates that the results of the numerical information comparison method are categorized into distinct types, which are essential for understanding the relationship between the numerical data from ECs and EHR."},
{"entailment_contradiction_and_not_comparable": "The objects \"entailment,\" \"contradiction,\" and \"not comparable\" are the distinct categories into which the entailment results are classified. \"Entailment\" indicates that the numerical information from ECs supports the data in EHR. \"Contradiction\" suggests that the numerical information from ECs conflicts with the data in EHR. \"Not comparable\" means that the units or values in the numerical information from ECs and EHR cannot be directly compared."},
{"entailment_ones": "The \"entailment\" ones refer to the results of a numerical information comparison method that infers the entailment between numerical information representations (NIR) learned from electronic claims (ECs) and numerical information in electronic health records (EHR). These results indicate a specific type of relationship where the numerical information in EHR is consistent with the NIR."},
{"entailment_ones__should_be_regarded_as__supported_by_the_numerical_information_entailment_module": "The phrase \"should be regarded as\" indicates a recommendation or a necessary interpretation of the \"entailment\" results. It suggests that these results need to be viewed or treated in a certain way, specifically as being validated or confirmed by a particular module."},
{"supported_by_the_numerical_information_entailment_module": "The \"supported by the numerical information entailment module\" refers to the validation or confirmation provided by a specific system or component designed to handle and process numerical information entailment. This module is responsible for ensuring that the \"entailment\" results are accurate and reliable by comparing numerical data from different sources."},
