{"following_a_procedure": "The procedure followed to generate the synthetic data is a set of steps or methods designed to create data that closely resembles real data. This procedure ensures that the synthetic data is relevant and useful for the intended purpose, such as testing machine learning models."},
{"clinical_trial_data": "Clinical trial data refers to the information and results obtained from clinical trials, which are research studies performed on human participants to evaluate the safety and efficacy of medical interventions. In this context, the clinical trial data consists of 1,000 trial descriptions that have been gathered for analysis."},
{"clinical_trial_data__downloaded_from__https_clinicaltrials_gov": "The predicate \"downloaded from\" indicates the action of obtaining or retrieving the clinical trial data from a specific source. In this case, the data was acquired by downloading it from a designated website."},
{"https_clinicaltrials_gov": "The object \"https://clinicaltrials.gov\" is a website that serves as a registry and results database of privately and publicly funded clinical trials conducted around the world. It is a valuable resource for accessing detailed information about clinical trials, including their objectives, methodologies, and outcomes."},
{"clinical_trial_descriptions": "Clinical trial descriptions refer to detailed documentations of clinical trials, which are systematic studies conducted to determine the safety and efficacy of medical interventions. These descriptions are crucial for researchers, healthcare providers, and patients to understand the methodology, outcomes, and implications of the trials."},
{"clinical_trial_descriptions__number__1_000": "The predicate 'number' in this context indicates the total count or quantity of clinical trial descriptions that have been gathered or are available for analysis. It serves as a quantifier to provide an overview of the dataset's size."},
{"1_000": "The object '1,000' represents the specific quantity of clinical trial descriptions that were downloaded from the https://clinicaltrials.gov website. This number signifies the scale of the dataset used for further analysis or research."},
{"each_trial__stored_in__an_xml_file": "The predicate \"stored in\" indicates the method or format in which the data from each clinical trial is preserved and organized. It suggests a systematic approach to data management, ensuring that each trial's information is accessible and structured."},
{"an_xml_file": "An XML file is a type of file format used for storing data with a structured, hierarchical format. In the context of clinical trials, each trial's data is encapsulated within an XML file, adhering to a predefined XML schema that outlines the structure of the fields within the file."},
{"xml_file": "An XML file is a data file marked up in Extensible Markup Language (XML) format, which is used to store and transport data. In the context of clinical trials, each trial's description is stored in an XML file."},
{"xml_file__follows__a_structure_of_fields_defined_by_an_xml_schema": "The predicate \"follows\" indicates that the XML file adheres to or is compliant with a specific set of rules or guidelines. In this case, it means the XML file is structured according to the defined fields specified by an XML schema."},
{"a_structure_of_fields_defined_by_an_xml_schema": "An XML schema defines the structure, content, and format of an XML document. It specifies the elements and attributes that can appear in a document, their types, and the relationships between them. In the context provided, the XML schema defines the structure of fields for clinical trial descriptions."},
{"ec_items": "EC items refer to specific elements or components extracted from clinical trial descriptions, formatted in free-text. These items are crucial for analyzing and understanding the details of clinical trials.|Here is another description: EC items refer to specific elements or components within clinical trial descriptions that are extracted for analysis. These items are part of a larger dataset derived from XML files obtained from clinicaltrials.gov.|Here is another description: EC items refer to elements or components within electronic clinical (EC) records that are systematically categorized for better management and analysis within electronic health record (EHR) systems."},
{"ec_items__selected_from__xml_files": "The predicate \"selected from\" indicates the process of choosing or extracting specific data elements, in this case, EC items, from a larger dataset or source, which in this context are XML files containing clinical trial descriptions."},
{"ec_items__format__free_text": "The predicate \"format\" describes the manner in which the EC items are presented or stored. In this context, it specifies that the EC items are in a particular type of format that allows for certain characteristics or processing methods."},
{"free_text": "Free-text format indicates that the EC items are stored or presented as unstructured text. This means that the data is not confined to specific fields or categories but is instead written in a narrative or descriptive style, allowing for a wide range of expressions and details."},
{"original_dataset": "The original dataset refers to the collection of clinical trial descriptions sourced from clinicaltrials.gov. It is comprised of XML files that adhere to a predefined schema, encapsulating various fields relevant to clinical trials."},
{"original_dataset__consists_of__ec_items_in_free_text_format": "The phrase \"consists of\" indicates the composition or makeup of the original dataset. It specifies the elements or types of data that are included within the dataset."},
{"ec_items_in_free_text_format": "The EC items in free-text format are specific components extracted from the XML files of clinical trial descriptions. These items are in a textual format, as opposed to structured or coded data, and are part of the original dataset used for analysis or processing."},
{"ec_items__could_be_divided_into__demographic_information_condition_occurrence_procedure_occurrence_measurement_drug_exposure_observation_and_willing_information": "The phrase \"could be divided into\" indicates a classification or categorization process where EC items are organized into distinct groups based on their characteristics or the type of information they represent."},
{"demographic_information_condition_occurrence_procedure_occurrence_measurement_drug_exposure_observation_and_willing_information": "The categories into which EC items are divided include demographic information, condition occurrence, procedure occurrence, measurement, drug exposure, observation, and willing information. These categories help in structuring and retrieving specific types of clinical data efficiently within EHR systems."},
{"other_categories": "Other categories refer to the various types of electronic clinical (EC) items that are not classified as willing or demographic information. These categories include condition occurrence, procedure occurrence, measurement, drug exposure, and observation."},
{"other_categories__can_be_mapped_with__corresponding_tables_in_ehr_system": "The phrase \"can be mapped with\" indicates a relationship where the subject categories have a direct correspondence or alignment with specific elements in another system. In this context, it means that these categories can be directly associated or linked with certain tables within the EHR (Electronic Health Record) system."},
{"corresponding_tables_in_ehr_system": "Corresponding tables in the EHR system are specific data tables within the electronic health record system that align with the various categories of EC items. These tables store patient-level information related to condition occurrence, procedure occurrence, measurement, drug exposure, and observation, allowing for the generation of EHR queries and the retrieval of relevant patient data."},
{"ec_record": "The EC record refers to an electronic case report that contains various types of patient information such as demographic details, condition occurrences, procedure occurrences, measurements, drug exposures, and observations. These records are essential for generating corresponding EHR queries."},
{"ec_record__use__criteria2query_system": "The use of the Criteria2Query system in the context of EC records involves transforming the information contained within these records into structured EHR queries. This process is crucial for mapping EC items to corresponding tables in an EHR system and generating patient-level EHR information."},
{"criteria2query_system": "The Criteria2Query system is a tool designed to convert electronic case report (EC) data into structured queries for electronic health records (EHR). It facilitates the generation of EHR queries in a specific format (CDM v5.0) by categorizing EC items and extracting relevant EHR query information, which can then be executed in an EHR query engine like ATLAS.|Here is another description: The Criteria2Query system is a tool designed to convert electronic case report (EC) records into executable queries for electronic health record (EHR) systems. It is specifically tailored to handle various categories of EC items, excluding willing and demographic information, by mapping them to corresponding tables within the EHR system."},
{"criteria2query_system__generate__a_corresponding_ehr_query_in_cdm_v5_0_format": "The action 'generate' in this context refers to the process by which the Criteria2Query system creates a specific type of query. This involves translating the structured data from EC records into a format that can be directly used to query an EHR system, ensuring that the query is compatible with the system's data model and can retrieve relevant patient-level information."},
{"a_corresponding_ehr_query_in_cdm_v5_0_format": "A corresponding EHR query in CDM v5.0 format is the output produced by the Criteria2Query system. This query is designed to be executed within an EHR system that adheres to the Common Data Model (CDM) version 5.0. It includes specific category labels for the EC items and the necessary query information to retrieve patient data that matches the criteria specified in the EC records."},
{"ehr_query": "The EHR query is a structured request generated by the Criteria2Query system in CDM v5.0 format, designed to extract specific patient-level information from an EHR system."},
{"ehr_query__includes__the_category_label_for_ec_and_the_ehr_query_information": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the EHR query contains or incorporates specific elements within its structure. In this context, it means the query is designed to encompass certain categories and information relevant to the EC record."},
{"the_category_label_for_ec_and_the_ehr_query_information": "The category label for EC and the EHR query information refers to the specific classification of the EC item (such as condition occurrence, procedure occurrence, etc.) and the detailed query parameters needed to retrieve corresponding data from the EHR system."},
{"queries": "Queries in this context refer to EHR (Electronic Health Record) queries generated in CDM v5.0 format. These queries are created from EC (Eligibility Criteria) records using the Criteria2Query system and are designed to extract specific patient-level EHR information."},
{"queries__run_in__atlas": "The predicate \"run in\" describes the action of executing or processing the EHR queries within a specific system. In this case, it indicates that the generated queries are being executed within the ATLAS system to retrieve relevant patient data."},
{"atlas": "ATLAS is an online EHR query engine used to process and execute EHR queries. It is a tool that allows for the generation of eligible patient-level EHR information by running the queries created from EC records. The resulting information is then stored in a relational database.|Here is another description: ATLAS is a tool used in the process of generating and querying electronic health records (EHRs). It is specifically mentioned as part of a system that translates clinical data into natural text."},
{"atlas__is__an_online_ehr_query_engine": "The predicate \"is\" serves as a simple state of being verb, indicating that ATLAS functions as or is classified as a particular type of system, in this case, an online EHR query engine."},
{"an_online_ehr_query_engine": "An online EHR query engine is a system designed to search and retrieve patient-level electronic health record information from a database. ATLAS, as described in the context, is used to run queries that generate eligible patient-level EHR information, which is then stored and potentially translated into natural text."},
{"eligible_patient_level_ehr_information": "Eligible patient-level EHR information refers to the specific data extracted from electronic health records that meet certain criteria or conditions. This data is derived from various categories such as condition occurrence, procedure occurrence, measurement, drug exposure, and observation, and is generated through queries run in an EHR query engine like ATLAS."},
{"eligible_patient_level_ehr_information__are_stored_in__relational_database": "The phrase \"are stored in\" indicates the action of placing or keeping the eligible patient-level EHR information within a designated system or structure. In this context, it signifies that the generated EHR data is saved and organized within a specific type of database."},
{"relational_database": "A relational database is a type of database that stores and organizes data in tables that are related to one another. It uses structured query language (SQL) to manage and retrieve data. In this context, the relational database is used to store the eligible patient-level EHR information generated from the EHR query engine."},
{"algorithm": "The algorithm is a set of instructions or rules designed to perform a specific task, in this case, processing and converting data from a database into a more human-readable format."},
{"algorithm__translate__the_database_information_into_natural_text": "The predicate \"translate\" in this context refers to the process by which the algorithm converts complex database information into a simpler, more understandable natural text format. This involves interpreting the structured data and rephrasing it in a way that is accessible to non-technical users."},
{"the_database_information_into_natural_text": "The object \"the database information into natural text\" refers to the raw, structured data stored in a relational database that is being converted by the algorithm into a format that resembles natural language. This transformation makes the information easier to read and comprehend for individuals who may not be familiar with database structures or query languages."},
{"matching_ehr_and_ec": "Matching EHR and EC refers to the process of aligning electronic health record (EHR) data with eligibility criteria (EC) in clinical studies. This alignment is crucial for determining patient eligibility based on their health records."},
{"matching_ehr_and_ec__are_labelled_as__entailment_for_inclusion_criteria_and_contradiction_for_exclusion_criteria": "The phrase \"are labelled as\" indicates the action of assigning specific categories or labels to the matched EHR and EC pairs. This labeling is a critical step in organizing and interpreting the data for further analysis."},
{"entailment_for_inclusion_criteria_and_contradiction_for_exclusion_criteria": "Entailment for inclusion criteria and Contradiction for exclusion criteria are specific labels assigned to EHR and EC pairs. Entailment signifies that the EHR data meets the inclusion criteria, making the patient eligible for the study. Contradiction indicates that the EHR data does not meet the exclusion criteria, thus disqualifying the patient from the study."},
{"unrelated_ehr_and_ec_pairs": "Unrelated EHR and EC pairs refer to instances where electronic health records (EHR) and electronic case reports (EC) do not have a relevant or meaningful connection. These pairs are generated as part of a process to create a comprehensive dataset for evaluating the relationships between EHR and EC."},
{"unrelated_ehr_and_ec_pairs__are_also_generated_and_labelled_as__neutral": "The predicate \"are also generated and labelled as\" indicates a process where, in addition to creating related EHR and EC pairs, a set of unrelated pairs is produced. These unrelated pairs are then systematically categorized and marked with a specific label to distinguish them from the related pairs."},
{"labeling_process": "The labeling process is a method used to categorize and tag pairs of EHR (Electronic Health Record) and EC (Eligibility Criteria) as either Entailment, Contradiction, or Neutral. This process is crucial for preparing data for model input in the context of medical data analysis and processing."},
{"labeling_process__forms_tuples_in_the_form_of__e_h_y_as_model_input": "The predicate \"forms tuples in the form of\" describes the action taken by the labeling process to organize and structure the labeled EHR and EC pairs. This action involves creating structured data tuples that can be used as input for machine learning models."},
{"e_h_y_as_model_input": "The object \"( _E_, _H_, _y_ )\" represents the structured tuples created by the labeling process. Each tuple consists of three elements: _E_, which likely stands for the EHR data; _H_, which probably represents the EC data; and _y_, which denotes the label assigned to the pair (Entailment, Contradiction, or Neutral). These tuples serve as the input data for models designed to analyze and process medical information."},
{"anita_alicante_anna_corazza_francesco_isgr\u00f2_and_stefano_silvestri": "Anita Alicante, Anna Corazza, Francesco Isgr\u00f2, and Stefano Silvestri are researchers who collaborated on a study in the field of computational biology and medicine."},
