{"ipf": "IPF, or Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis, is a chronic and progressive lung disease characterized by scarring of the lung tissue, leading to difficulty in breathing and reduced oxygen intake.|Here is another description: IPF, or Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis, is a rare and progressive lung disease characterized by scarring of the lung tissue, leading to difficulty in breathing and reduced lung function. It is a condition with no known cure, and treatments aim to slow the progression of the disease and manage symptoms.|Here is another description: IPF, or Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis, is a rare and progressive lung disease characterized by scarring of the lung tissue, leading to difficulty in breathing and reduced oxygen intake. It affects a small percentage of the global population and currently has no known cure.|Here is another description: IPF, or Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis, is a rare and progressive lung disease characterized by scarring of lung tissue, leading to difficulty in breathing and reduced oxygen intake. It affects a small percentage of the global population, with a prevalence rate of 0.04%."},
{"ipf__is_a__rare_disease": "The phrase \"is a\" serves as a classification or identification link, indicating that the subject, IPF, belongs to a specific category or type, in this case, a rare disease."},
{"rare_disease": "A rare disease is a medical condition that affects a small percentage of the population, often defined as fewer than 200,000 people in the United States or 1 in 2,000 people in the European Union. These diseases can present unique challenges in diagnosis, treatment, and research due to their low prevalence."},
{"ipf__affects__approximately_5_million_people_worldwide": "The predicate 'affects' in this context signifies the impact or influence that IPF has on a specific number of individuals globally. It indicates the scale of the disease's occurrence and the population it impacts."},
{"approximately_5_million_people_worldwide": "'Approximately 5 million people worldwide' refers to the estimated number of individuals globally who are affected by IPF. This figure highlights the prevalence of the disease on an international scale, indicating the widespread nature of its impact."},
{"ipf__has_prevalence_rate__0_04": "The predicate 'has prevalence rate' indicates the proportion of a population that is affected by a particular disease or condition at a specific time. In the context of IPF, it quantifies how common the disease is among the worldwide population."},
{"0_04": "The object '0.04%' represents the prevalence rate of IPF, indicating that approximately 0.04% of the global population is affected by this rare lung disease. This small percentage underscores the rarity of IPF among the general population."},
{"ipf__has_no__cure": "The predicate \"has no\" in this context signifies the absence or lack of a specific treatment or solution for IPF. It highlights the current medical reality where a definitive cure is not available for this condition."},
{"cure": "\"Cure\" in this context refers to a medical treatment or intervention that would completely eliminate IPF from a patient's body, restoring normal lung function and alleviating all symptoms associated with the disease. Currently, such a cure does not exist."},
{"treatments": "Treatments refer to the medical interventions and therapies that are being researched and applied to manage a specific condition, in this case, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF).|Here is another description: Treatments refer to the medical interventions and therapies that are being researched and implemented to manage and alleviate the symptoms of Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF)."},
{"treatments__are_being_developed_and_tested__to_slow_the_rate_of_scarring_pirfenidone_and_nintedanib": "The phrase \"are being developed and tested\" indicates an ongoing process where treatments are in various stages of research, clinical trials, and evaluation to assess their effectiveness and safety for use in patients."},
{"to_slow_the_rate_of_scarring_pirfenidone_and_nintedanib": "The treatments aimed at slowing the rate of scarring, specifically pirfenidone and nintedanib, are medications that have been identified as potential therapies to reduce the progression of fibrosis in the lungs, which is a hallmark of IPF."},
{"treatments__are_being_developed_and_tested__to_treat_particular_ipf_symptoms_such_as_breathlessness_and_coughing": "The phrase \"are being developed and tested\" indicates the ongoing process of creating, evaluating, and refining treatments specifically designed to address the challenges posed by IPF."},
{"to_treat_particular_ipf_symptoms_such_as_breathlessness_and_coughing": "The treatments aim to alleviate specific symptoms of Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF), such as breathlessness and coughing, which are common and debilitating aspects of the disease."},
{"ec_statement": "The EC statement refers to a specific guideline or requirement set by an ethics committee or similar regulatory body for a clinical trial. In this context, it is related to the inclusion criteria for participants in a trial for Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF) treatments.|Here is another description: The EC statement refers to a specific guideline or requirement set by an ethics committee or similar regulatory body for a clinical trial. It outlines necessary conditions or criteria that participants must meet to be eligible for the trial."},
{"ec_statement__states__patients_should_have_history_of_cough": "The predicate'states' indicates that the EC statement explicitly declares or specifies certain conditions that must be met by participants in the clinical trial. It serves as a formal declaration of the necessary participant characteristics or medical history."},
{"patients_should_have_history_of_cough": "The object 'patients should have history of cough' specifies one of the inclusion criteria for participants in the IPF clinical trial. This criterion ensures that only patients who have experienced a cough as part of their medical history are eligible to participate in the trial, likely because coughing is a relevant symptom of IPF."},
{"ec_statement__states__patients_should_have_no_known_allergy_to_opeprazole_or_other_proton_pump_inhibitor": "The predicate \"states\" indicates that the EC statement explicitly declares or specifies certain conditions. In this context, it means the statement clearly outlines the requirements or exclusions for participants in the clinical trial."},
{"patients_should_have_no_known_allergy_to_opeprazole_or_other_proton_pump_inhibitor": "The object describes the specific condition that participants must meet according to the EC statement. It specifies that patients should not have a known allergy to Omeprazole or any other proton pump inhibitor, which is a crucial safety requirement for the trial."},
{"visualization": "Visualization refers to the process of creating graphical representations of data, information, or concepts to facilitate understanding and analysis. In the context of DeepEnroll, visualization is used to interpret and display complex data related to patient recruitment."},
{"visualization__concludes__attention_weights_in_deepenroll_help_better_understand_the_key_inclusion_and_exclusion_criteria_for_successful_recruitment_of_patients": "The predicate \"concludes\" indicates the action of reaching a final decision or judgment based on the presented data or analysis. In this context, it signifies the outcome derived from the visualization process, highlighting the insights gained about patient recruitment criteria."},
{"attention_weights_in_deepenroll_help_better_understand_the_key_inclusion_and_exclusion_criteria_for_successful_recruitment_of_patients": "The attention weights in DeepEnroll are specific values assigned to different criteria during the patient recruitment process. These weights help in determining the importance of each criterion, thereby aiding in a better understanding of the key inclusion and exclusion factors necessary for successful patient recruitment."},
{"case_study__focuses_on__trials_that_are_difficult_to_match_patients_with_machine_learning_models": "The phrase \"focuses on\" indicates that the case study directs its attention and analysis specifically towards a particular topic or issue, in this instance, the challenges associated with matching patients to machine learning models in trials."},
{"trials_that_are_difficult_to_match_patients_with_machine_learning_models": "The trials in question are research studies or experiments that face significant difficulties in aligning patients with machine learning models, highlighting the complexities and challenges in this specific application of technology in healthcare."},
{"experiments__involve__clinical_trials": "The predicate \"involve\" indicates that the experiments include or encompass clinical trials as a key component. This relationship suggests that clinical trials are a necessary part of the experiments, serving as the context or environment in which the machine learning models are tested and evaluated."},
{"machine_learning_models": "Machine learning models refer to algorithms and systems designed to learn patterns and make predictions or decisions based on data. In the context of clinical trials, these models are utilized to process and analyze patient data."},
{"machine_learning_models__use_for__matching_patients": "The predicate \"use for\" indicates the purpose or application of machine learning models in this context. It highlights that these models are employed specifically to perform a task, which in this case is matching patients to clinical trials."},
{"matching_patients": "Matching patients in the context of clinical trials involves identifying and selecting suitable candidates for participation based on predefined criteria. This process is crucial for ensuring the validity and effectiveness of the trial outcomes."},
{"trial__show__lower_than_30_correct_patient_trial_matching": "The trial has demonstrated a specific performance metric in patient-trial matching, indicating the effectiveness of machine learning models in this context."},
{"lower_than_30_correct_patient_trial_matching": "The performance metric shown by the trial is lower than 30% correct patient-trial matching, which suggests that the machine learning models DeepEnroll and Criteria2Query have struggled to accurately match patients to this particular trial."},
{"trial__use__deepenroll_or_criteria2query": "The predicate, use, indicates the application or employment of specific machine learning models within the context of the trial to achieve a particular objective, such as patient-trial matching."},
{"deepenroll_or_criteria2query": "The object, DeepEnroll or Criteria2Query, represents machine learning models utilized in the trial for the purpose of matching patients to the trial, though with noted challenges in achieving high accuracy."},
{"trial__name__blitz_heart_failure_nct03661060": "The predicate 'name' indicates the specific designation or title given to the trial, which is essential for identification and reference in clinical research and documentation."},
{"blitz_heart_failure_nct03661060": "BLITZ Heart Failure (NCT03661060) is the official name and identifier of the trial. This name is used to uniquely identify the study within the clinical trial registry and in scientific literature.|Here is another description: BLITZ Heart Failure (NCT03661060) is a clinical trial focused on heart failure patients. It aims to evaluate the effectiveness of a structured approach in managing heart failure."},
{"blitz_heart_failure_nct03661060__type__observational_study": "The predicate \"type\" in this context refers to the classification or category of the clinical trial. It indicates the nature or methodology employed in the trial."},
{"observational_study": "An observational study is a type of clinical research where investigators assess health outcomes in groups of participants according to a research protocol. It involves observing and analyzing data without intervening or altering the participants' treatments.|Here is another description: The observational study in question is the BLITZ Heart Failure trial (NCT03661060), which is a clinical trial aimed at analyzing patient data without intervention."},
{"observational_study__design__to_evaluate_whether_a_structured": "The design of the observational study refers to the structured approach and methodology implemented to systematically collect and analyze data in order to draw conclusions about the research question."},
{"to_evaluate_whether_a_structured": "The structured evaluation in the context of the observational study is a methodical approach intended to assess whether specific criteria or outcomes are met, in this case, related to patient-trial matching efficiency using machine learning models."},
{"deepenroll__encodes__enrollment_criteria_and_patients_records_into_a_shared_latent_space": "The term \"encodes\" in this context refers to the process by which DeepEnroll transforms and represents enrollment criteria and patient records in a unified, abstract form, facilitating efficient matching and analysis."},
{"enrollment_criteria_and_patients_records_into_a_shared_latent_space": "Enrollment criteria and patients' records, when encoded into a shared latent space, represent a unified data representation that allows for sophisticated analysis and matching between clinical trials and patient electronic health records (EHR). This shared space enables the model to draw meaningful connections and insights from disparate data sources."},
{"deepenroll__applies__a_pre_trained_bert_model_to_encode_clinical_trial_information": "The predicate \"applies\" in this context signifies the utilization or implementation of a specific method or model component within the DeepEnroll framework to process and encode data relevant to clinical trials."},
{"a_pre_trained_bert_model_to_encode_clinical_trial_information": "A pre-trained BERT model is employed within the DeepEnroll framework to encode clinical trial information. BERT, which stands for Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers, is a powerful natural language processing model that can understand the context of words in a sentence, making it suitable for encoding complex textual data like clinical trial information."},
{"deepenroll__uses__a_hierarchical_model_to_embed_patient_ehr": "The term \"uses\" in this context signifies the application or incorporation of a specific method or model by DeepEnroll to achieve its objectives. It indicates the operational dependency of DeepEnroll on certain techniques to process and analyze data effectively."},
{"a_hierarchical_model_to_embed_patient_ehr": "A hierarchical model to embed patient EHR refers to a structured approach used by DeepEnroll to convert electronic health records into a format that can be analyzed and understood within the model's framework. This model likely involves multiple layers of processing to capture the complexity and nuances of patient data."},
{"numerical_information_embedding_and_entailment_module__matches__numerical_information_in_ec_and_ehr": "The term \"matches\" in this context refers to the process by which the numerical information embedding and entailment module aligns and compares numerical data from enrollment criteria with that from electronic health records to find correspondences."},
{"deepenroll__was_evaluated_on__both_real_world_clinical_trial_dataset_and_a_synthetic_data": "The predicate \"was evaluated on\" indicates that DeepEnroll underwent assessment and testing to determine its performance and effectiveness. This evaluation process is crucial to validate the model's capabilities and ensure its reliability in practical applications."},
{"both_real_world_clinical_trial_dataset_and_a_synthetic_data": "The objects of evaluation for DeepEnroll include both a real-world clinical trial dataset and a synthetic data set. The real-world dataset consists of actual clinical trial information and patient records, while the synthetic data set is artificially generated to simulate real-world scenarios. These datasets provide a comprehensive testing ground to assess the model's performance across different types of data."},
{"experiments__demonstrated__the_strong_performance_of_deepenroll": "Demonstrated indicates that the experiments provided clear evidence or proof of the model's capabilities and effectiveness."},
{"the_strong_performance_of_deepenroll": "The strong performance of DeepEnroll refers to the effective and superior results achieved by the DeepEnroll model in the experiments, showcasing its capability in patient-trial matching based on heterogeneous data."},
{"deepenroll_method": "DeepEnroll is a cross-modal inference learning model designed to encode enrollment criteria and patient records into a shared latent space, facilitating patient-trial matching. It utilizes a pre-trained BERT model for clinical trial information and a hierarchical model for embedding patient EHR, enhanced by a numerical information embedding and entailment module."},
{"deepenroll_method__can_be_extended_to__other_application_domains_for_matching_problems_based_on_heterogeneous_data": "The phrase \"can be extended to\" indicates the potential adaptability and versatility of the DeepEnroll method. It suggests that the method is not limited to its original application but can be modified or expanded to fit other contexts or problems."},
{"other_application_domains_for_matching_problems_based_on_heterogeneous_data": "Other application domains for matching problems based on heterogeneous data refer to various fields where data of different types and sources need to be matched or aligned. Examples include personalized targeted advertising, where text descriptions of ads are matched with customers' browsing histories, which are sequential data."},
{"other_application_domains": "Other application domains refer to areas beyond the primary focus of the paper, which involves patient-trial matching using the DeepEnroll model. These domains could involve different types of data matching problems."},
{"other_application_domains__include__personalized_targeted_advertising": "The term 'include' signifies that the subject, other application domains, encompasses or contains the object, personalized targeted advertising, as one of the potential uses or extensions of the proposed method."},
{"personalized_targeted_advertising": "Personalized targeted advertising is a marketing strategy that uses data about individual customers, such as their browsing history, to deliver tailored advertisements. This approach relies on matching ad descriptions with customer data to increase the relevance and effectiveness of the ads shown."},
{"this_work": "This work refers to a research project or academic endeavor that has received financial support from various funding bodies.|Here is another description: This work refers to a specific research project or study that has received financial support from various funding bodies."},
{"this_work__is_part_supported_by__national_science_foundation_award_iis_1418511_ccf_1533768_and_iis_1838042": "The phrase \"is part supported by\" indicates that the research project or academic work has received partial financial backing from the mentioned funding sources."},
{"national_science_foundation_award_iis_1418511_ccf_1533768_and_iis_1838042": "The National Science Foundation awards IIS-1418511, CCF-1533768, and IIS-1838042 are grants provided by the National Science Foundation to support research in information and intelligent systems, computer and communication foundations, and other related fields."},
{"this_work__is_part_supported_by__the_national_institute_of_health_award_nih_r01_1r01ns107291_01_and_r56hl138415": "The phrase \"is part supported by\" indicates that the research project or study has received partial financial backing from the mentioned funding sources, implying that there are other sources of support as well."},
{"the_national_institute_of_health_award_nih_r01_1r01ns107291_01_and_r56hl138415": "The National Institute of Health award NIH R01 1R01NS107291-01 and R56HL138415 are specific grants awarded by the National Institute of Health to support the research project or study. These grants are part of the overall funding that the project has received."},
{"model_reproducibility": "Model reproducibility refers to the ability to consistently achieve the same results when running a machine learning model under the same conditions. It is crucial for validating the performance and reliability of machine learning algorithms."},
{"model_reproducibility__requires__synthetic_data": "Requires indicates a necessary condition or component needed for the subject to function or be achieved. In this context, it signifies that model reproducibility cannot be attained without the specified object."},
{"synthetic_data__was_generated__following_a_procedure": "The phrase \"was generated\" indicates the process by which the synthetic data came into existence. It suggests that the data was created through a deliberate and systematic approach, following a predefined set of steps or rules."},
{"following_a_procedure": "The procedure refers to a series of steps or methods followed to create the synthetic data. This procedure is crucial for ensuring that the data is consistent, reliable, and reproducible, allowing researchers to test and validate their models effectively."},
{"clinical_trial_data": "Clinical trial data refers to the information and results obtained from clinical trials, which are research studies performed on human participants to evaluate the safety and efficacy of medical interventions. In this context, the clinical trial data consists of 1,000 trial descriptions that have been gathered for analysis."},
{"clinical_trial_data__downloaded_from__https_clinicaltrials_gov": "The predicate \"downloaded from\" indicates the action of obtaining or retrieving the clinical trial data from a specific source. In this case, the data was acquired by downloading it from the website https://clinicaltrials.gov."},
{"https_clinicaltrials_gov": "The object \"https://clinicaltrials.gov\" is a website that serves as a registry and results database of privately and publicly funded clinical studies conducted around the world. It is a comprehensive resource for accessing clinical trial information, including trial descriptions, participant criteria, locations, and outcomes."},
{"clinical_trial_descriptions": "Clinical trial descriptions refer to detailed documentations of clinical trials, which are systematic studies conducted to determine the safety and efficacy of medical interventions. These descriptions are crucial for researchers, healthcare professionals, and regulatory bodies to understand the methodology, outcomes, and implications of the trials."},
{"clinical_trial_descriptions__number__1_000": "The predicate 'number' in this context indicates the total count or quantity of clinical trial descriptions that have been gathered or are available for analysis. It serves as a quantifier to provide an understanding of the scale or volume of data being considered."},
{"1_000": "The object '1,000' represents the specific quantity of clinical trial descriptions that were downloaded from the website https://clinicaltrials.gov. This number signifies the extent of the dataset used for further analysis or study, highlighting the comprehensiveness of the data collection effort."},
{"each_trial__stored_in__an_xml_file": "The phrase \"stored in\" indicates the method or format in which the clinical trial data is preserved and organized. It suggests a systematic approach to data management, ensuring that each trial's information is kept in a structured and accessible manner."},
{"an_xml_file": "An XML file is a type of file that uses a set of rules to encode documents in machine-readable form. In the context of clinical trials, each trial's data is encoded following a structure of fields defined by an XML schema, which allows for the systematic organization and retrieval of trial information."},
{"xml_file": "An XML file is a type of file that uses a set of tags to define elements and their relationships, allowing for the structured storage and exchange of data. In the context of clinical trials, each trial's description is encapsulated within an XML file."},
{"xml_file__follows__a_structure_of_fields_defined_by_an_xml_schema": "The term \"follows\" in this context indicates that the XML file adheres to or is compliant with a predefined set of rules or guidelines. Specifically, it means that the XML file is organized according to a specified structure."},
{"a_structure_of_fields_defined_by_an_xml_schema": "The structure of fields defined by an XML schema is a formal description of the elements and their relationships that an XML file must contain. This schema ensures that the XML file is consistent and can be correctly interpreted by software applications."},
{"ec_items": "EC items refer to specific elements or components within clinical trial descriptions that are of interest for analysis or processing. These items are extracted in free-text format from the downloaded XML files.|Here is another description: EC items refer to specific elements or components within clinical trial descriptions that are extracted for analysis. These items are part of a larger dataset derived from XML files obtained from clinicaltrials.gov.|Here is another description: EC items refer to elements or components within electronic clinical (EC) records that are systematically organized and categorized for better management and analysis in electronic health record (EHR) systems."},
{"ec_items__selected_from__xml_files": "The predicate \"selected from\" indicates the process of choosing or extracting specific EC items from a larger set of data. In this context, it describes how EC items are carefully picked from the XML files to form the original dataset for further analysis."},
{"ec_items__format__free_text": "The format predicate indicates the manner in which the EC items are presented or stored. In this context, it specifies that the EC items are in a particular type of format."},
{"free_text": "Free-text format refers to a type of data representation where information is stored in plain, unstructured text. This means that the EC items are not confined to specific fields or categories but are instead presented as continuous, readable text."},
{"original_dataset": "The original dataset refers to the initial collection of data gathered for analysis or processing. In this context, it specifically denotes the set of data extracted from clinical trial descriptions downloaded from a specified source."},
{"original_dataset__consists_of__ec_items_in_free_text_format": "The phrase \"consists of\" indicates the composition or makeup of the original dataset. It suggests that the dataset is made up of specific elements or types of data."},
{"ec_items_in_free_text_format": "The EC items in free-text format are specific components within the original dataset. These items are extracted from XML files that store clinical trial descriptions and are formatted in a free-text manner, meaning they are not constrained by a rigid structure and can include various types of textual information."},
{"ec_items__could_be_divided_into__demographic_information_condition_occurrence_procedure_occurrence_measurement_drug_exposure_observation_and_willing_information": "The phrase \"could be divided into\" indicates a classification or categorization process where a broader category is segmented into more specific subcategories for detailed analysis or organization."},
{"demographic_information_condition_occurrence_procedure_occurrence_measurement_drug_exposure_observation_and_willing_information": "The object of the triple represents various categories into which EC items can be divided. These include demographic information, condition occurrence, procedure occurrence, measurement, drug exposure, observation, and willing information, each representing different types of data collected in clinical settings."},
{"other_categories": "Other categories refer to the various types of electronic clinical (EC) items that are not classified under willing or demographic information. These categories include condition occurrence, procedure occurrence, measurement, drug exposure, and observation."},
{"other_categories__can_be_mapped_with__corresponding_tables_in_ehr_system": "The phrase \"can be mapped with\" indicates a relationship where the subject categories have a direct correspondence or alignment with specific elements in another system. In this context, it means that these categories can be directly associated with or translated into specific tables within an electronic health record (EHR) system."},
{"corresponding_tables_in_ehr_system": "Corresponding tables in the EHR system are specific data structures within the electronic health record system that store information related to the various categories of EC items. These tables are designed to hold data such as condition occurrences, procedure occurrences, measurements, drug exposures, and observations, allowing for the integration and management of clinical data within the EHR framework."},
{"ec_record": "The EC record refers to an electronic case report that contains various types of patient information, such as condition occurrence, procedure occurrence, measurement, drug exposure, and observation. This record is a structured format used in clinical data management."},
{"ec_record__generates__a_corresponding_ehr_query_in_cdm_v5_0_format": "The predicate 'generates' describes the process by which an EC record is transformed or utilized to create something else. In this context, it specifically refers to the action of converting an EC record into a query format that can be understood and processed by an electronic health record (EHR) system."},
{"a_corresponding_ehr_query_in_cdm_v5_0_format": "A corresponding EHR query in CDM v5.0 format is a structured query derived from an EC record, formatted according to the Common Data Model version 5.0. This query is designed to be compatible with EHR systems, allowing for the retrieval of specific patient-level information from the EHR database."},
{"ehr_query": "An EHR query is a structured request sent to an electronic health record system to retrieve specific patient data. It is generated based on predefined criteria and is used to extract relevant information from the EHR system for further analysis or processing."},
{"ehr_query__includes__the_category_label_for_ec_and_the_ehr_query_information": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the EHR query contains or incorporates specific elements within its structure. In this context, it indicates that the EHR query is designed to encompass certain categories and information pertinent to the electronic case report (EC)."},
{"the_category_label_for_ec_and_the_ehr_query_information": "The category label for EC and the EHR query information refers to the specific classification and details included within the EHR query. These elements are essential for accurately mapping EC items to corresponding tables in the EHR system and for generating relevant patient-level EHR information."},
{"queries": "Queries refer to the EHR queries generated in CDM v5.0 format by the Criteria2Query system, which are based on EC records and mapped to corresponding tables in the EHR system."},
{"queries__run_in__atlas_github_com_ohdsi_atlas": "The predicate 'run in' describes the action of executing or processing the generated EHR queries within a specific system or engine, in this case, ATLAS."},
{"atlas_github_com_ohdsi_atlas": "ATLAS (github.com/OHDSI/Atlas) is an online EHR query engine where the generated EHR queries are run to extract eligible patient-level EHR information, which is then stored in a relational database."},
{"eligible_patient_level_ehr_information": "Eligible patient-level EHR information refers to the specific data extracted from electronic health records that meet certain criteria or conditions. This information is crucial for various healthcare analyses and decision-making processes."},
{"eligible_patient_level_ehr_information__generated_by__running_queries_in_atlas": "The phrase \"generated by\" indicates the process or method through which the subject (eligible patient-level EHR information) is produced. In this context, it signifies that the information is created as a result of executing certain operations or queries."},
{"running_queries_in_atlas": "Running queries in ATLAS refers to the act of executing specific search requests within the ATLAS system, an online electronic health record (EHR) query engine. This process is designed to retrieve and generate relevant patient-level EHR information based on the criteria set by the queries."},
{"information": "The subject, 'Information,' refers to the data generated from EHR queries in the context of electronic health records (EHR). This includes various categories such as condition occurrence, procedure occurrence, measurement, drug exposure, and observation, which are derived from patient-level EHR data."},
{"information__stored_in__relational_database": "The predicate,'stored in,' describes the process of saving or keeping the generated EHR information within a specific type of database. This action implies that the information is organized and maintained in a structured format for easy retrieval and analysis."},
{"relational_database": "The object,'relational database,' is a type of database that organizes data into tables with rows and columns. In this context, it is used to store the EHR information generated from queries, allowing for efficient data management and retrieval."},
{"algorithm": "The algorithm is a set of instructions or rules designed to perform a specific task, in this case, processing and converting data from a database into a more human-readable format."},
{"algorithm__developed_to__translate_the_database_information_into_natural_text": "The phrase \"developed to\" indicates the purpose or goal for which the algorithm was created. It signifies that the algorithm was specifically designed and implemented with the intention of achieving a particular outcome or functionality."},
{"translate_the_database_information_into_natural_text": "Translating database information into natural text involves converting structured data stored in a database into a format that is easily understandable by humans. This process typically involves generating sentences or phrases that describe the data in a coherent and meaningful way."},
{"matching_ehr_and_ec": "Matching EHR and EC refers to the process of aligning electronic health record (EHR) data with eligibility criteria (EC) in a clinical study. This alignment is crucial for determining patient eligibility based on their health records."},
{"matching_ehr_and_ec__labelled_as__entailment_for_inclusion_criteria_and_contradiction_for_exclusion_criteria": "The predicate \"labelled as\" indicates the action of assigning a specific category or status to the matched EHR and EC pairs. This categorization is essential for the subsequent analysis and decision-making processes in clinical studies."},
{"entailment_for_inclusion_criteria_and_contradiction_for_exclusion_criteria": "Entailment for inclusion criteria and Contradiction for exclusion criteria are the labels assigned to the matched EHR and EC pairs. Entailment signifies that the EHR data meets the inclusion criteria, making the patient eligible for the study. Contradiction indicates that the EHR data does not meet the exclusion criteria, thereby disqualifying the patient from the study."},
{"unrelated_ehr_and_ec_pairs": "Unrelated EHR and EC pairs refer to instances where electronic health records (EHR) and electronic criteria (EC) do not have a relevant or meaningful connection. These pairs are created to serve as neutral examples in the dataset."},
{"unrelated_ehr_and_ec_pairs__generated_and_labelled_as__neutral": "The predicate \"generated and labelled as\" describes the process by which unrelated EHR and EC pairs are created and subsequently categorized. This involves using a system to generate EHR queries from EC records and then running these queries to obtain patient-level EHR information, which is stored and translated into natural text. The pairs are then labeled based on their relevance."},
{"labeling_process": "The labeling process is a method used to categorize and mark matching electronic health records (EHR) and eligibility criteria (EC) as either Entailment, Contradiction, or Neutral. This process is crucial for preparing data for model input in the context of medical research and data analysis."},
{"labeling_process__forms_tuples_in_the_form_of__e_h_y_as_model_input": "The predicate \"forms tuples in the form of\" describes the action taken by the labeling process to organize and structure the data. It indicates that the labeling process creates structured data sets, specifically tuples, which are then used as input for models."},
{"e_h_y_as_model_input": "The object \"( _E_, _H_, _y_ )\" represents the structured tuples created by the labeling process. These tuples consist of three elements: _E_, which likely stands for eligibility criteria; _H_, which probably represents health records or data; and _y_, which is the label indicating the relationship between _E_ and _H_ (Entailment, Contradiction, or Neutral). These tuples serve as the input for models in the analysis of medical data."},
{"anita_alicante_anna_corazza_francesco_isgr\u00f2_and_stefano_silvestri": "Anita Alicante, Anna Corazza, Francesco Isgr\u00f2, and Stefano Silvestri are researchers who collaborated on a study involving natural language processing techniques applied to medical data."},
